nts (Frames 11–12, 21–22).\n  - Mounting black plastic/metal covers or housings onto the engine (Frames 23–28).\n  - Handling valve train components (Frames 29–30).\n\n---\n\n### ❌ 3) Engine/automotive parts — specifically turbochargers:\n- **NOT CONFIRMED.** The frames show **no turbochargers** being installed.\n  - The components being handled are **camshafts, valve springs, rocker arms, and engine covers** — all part of the **valvetrain and cylinder head assembly**.\n  - Turbochargers are large, distinct components with compressor and turbine housings, often with large inlet/outlet pipes — **none of these are visible** in any frame.\n  - Frame 13–15 show what appears to be an intake manifold or exhaust manifold section, but **not turbochargers**.\n  - The engine is being assembled from the top down — typical for valvetrain installation — not turbocharger mounting, which usually occurs on the sides or rear of the engine block.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ 4) Factory/workshop setting:\n- **Confirmed.** The background includes tool cabinets, shelving with parts, clean workbenches, and industrial lighting — consistent with a high-precision automotive assembly workshop (e.g., Bugatti Atelier in Molsheim).\n\n---\n\n### ✅ 5) Tools/machinery in use:\n- **Confirmed.** Workers use:\n  - Hand tools (e.g., screwdrivers, alignment tools — Frames 11–12, 21–22).\n  - Engine stands or fixtures to hold the engine block (Frames 1–5, 16–20).\n  - No heavy machinery, but precision hand tools are clearly in use.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ 6) Multiple workers collaborating:\n- **NOT CONFIRMED.** All frames show **only one worker at a time** handling the engine. No evidence of teamwork or collaboration in these specific frames.\n\n---\n\n## 🟡 Final Verdict: Is the answer “They are installing the turbochargers” correct?\n\n> **No, it is incorrect.**\n\nThe visual evidence clearly shows workers assembling the **valvetrain components** — specifically **camshafts, valve springs, and cylinder head covers** — **no